WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.670
Avengers: Endgame took secrecy to all new
levels with its intentionally spoiler-free

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:05.500
marketing efforts.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.580
Once the movie was released, though, the behind-the-scenes
info began pouring out.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.420
Here are some of the most interesting things
to emerge.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.900
It's a well-known fact that directors Joe
and Anthony Russo will go to extreme lengths

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.100
to keep their Marvel creations under wraps.

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:23.860
For example, they teasingly apologized to
the spoiler-prone Tom Holland for dusting

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:28.180
Spidey at the end of Infinity War and consequently
keeping him at arm's length for the promotion

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:28.760
of Endgame.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.460
They also pretended to fire Mark Ruffalo after
a string of spoilers had put him on thin ice.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.450
One of the best methods for keeping everyone
in the dark, though, was the way the Russos

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:38.280
handled the script.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.440
Most of the actors only got pieces of the
screenplay to work from, and apparently, Ruffalo

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.440
got a particularly spoiler-proof version of
the script.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.090
The Hulk actor was only given a partial script
with additional dummy scenes in it, according

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:52.300
to an interview with E! News:

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.180
"I don't know what's gonna happen.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.660
I shot like five different endings.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.640
I didn't even get a whole script for this
movie."

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.340
But even that didn't stop him from accidentally
giving something away.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.640
In that same interview, he joked about what
he presumably thought was one of those "dummy

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:10.140
scenes."

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.420
The spoiler?

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:12.400
"He gets married in this!"

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.180
"They have to throw Mark off the scent."

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:15.680
Oops…

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.540
Endgame is the second MCU outing for Carol
Danvers, who arrived on the scene just weeks

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.540
earlier in her own superhero flick, Captain
Marvel.

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.700
For actress Brie Larson, though, that wasn't
the case.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:31.319
Larson had filmed her scenes for Endgame before
she ever had a chance to flesh out the character

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:31.880
in her own film.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.740
As an added twist, the first scene she ever
filmed was set during the events of Endgame,

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.320
but technically ended up in Captain Marvel.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.640
On The Tonight Show, Larson recalled that
her first day of filming was for the end credit

00:01:43.649 --> 00:01:47.570
scene for her own film, which showed her arriving
at the Avengers facility post-snap.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.460
"Whip pans to me and I go, 'Where's Fury?'

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.130
I didn't know that was the tag for Captain
Marvel.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:54.069
I didn't know what that was that we were shooting.

00:01:54.069 --> 00:01:55.789
There was also no one else there."

00:01:55.789 --> 00:01:59.809
In true Russo fashion, though, the actress
was only given her lines and filmed her part

00:01:59.809 --> 00:02:01.240
alone in front of a green screen.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.940
That's taking secrecy to a whole new level.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:08.219
While the Russo brothers bent over backwards
to keep actors like Ruffalo, Holland, and

00:02:08.219 --> 00:02:12.299
Larson from spoiling the script, there was
one person that they didn't mind showing the

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.140
whole thing to: Robert Downey Jr.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.600
While discussing Infinity War and Endgame
in an interview with Rotten Tomatoes, the

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:20.290
pair talked about keeping things secret:

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:23.020
"RDJ was probably the only one to actually
read the entire script."

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:24.800
"Evans might have read the script."

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.100
"Part of our motivation to do that is that
it just takes a lot of pressure off of people."

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.800
Everything had started with Iron Man just
a decade earlier.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:35.530
With Endgame ultimately serving as the Iron
Avenger's swan song, it's only appropriate

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:39.980
that RDJ would be trusted with some of the
most sensitive MCU knowledge to ever exist.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.860
When the Avengers break into teams for their
time heist in Endgame, Rocket and Thor are

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:48.720
sent to Asgard during the events of Thor:
The Dark World to retrieve the Reality Stone.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:53.140
At that point in time, the Stone is using
the God of Thunder's ex, Jane Foster, as a

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:53.640
host.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.680
Foster, who's been absent from Marvel films
since The Dark World, appears onscreen a few

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.980
times before Rocket is able to get the Reality
Stone out of her.

00:03:01.609 --> 00:03:05.920
But it turns out that Foster actress Natalie
Portman didn't actually film any new footage

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:06.420
for Endgame.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.820
In an interview with Entertainment Weekly,
the Russos explained that they had been able

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:14.280
to salvage unused footage from The Dark World
in order to get all of the scenes they needed.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.810
While the footage was enough for them to work
with, they did ask the actress to record a

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:19.920
small bit of voice-over.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:24.900
In the days after Endgame lit up the theater
marquees, Star-Lord actor Chris Pratt stirred

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:29.189
up attention by releasing a video he'd taken
on set while preparing for one of the film's

00:03:29.189 --> 00:03:30.180
key action sequences.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:35.180
The once-in-a-lifetime video showed nearly
all of the famous actors, mostly in sweats

00:03:35.189 --> 00:03:39.170
and comfortable clothing, chatting and discussing
their scenes as casually as if they were a

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:41.340
group of high schoolers hanging out after
class.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.180
The video gave a unique glimpse into the inner
workings of a film that had been kept tightly

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:47.520
under wraps since day one.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.500
Stan Lee was a comic book titan for over half
a century.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:55.720
While he was an important creative mind behind
the scenes for decades, his long relationship

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.200
with Marvel also led to a memorable series
of fan-favorite cameos throughout various

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.280
Marvel movies.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.940
When Lee passed away in late 2018, fans began
theorizing about when his last cameo would

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:06.440
surface.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.840
A year earlier, it had been revealed that
Lee had filmed five different cameos for upcoming

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:11.340
films.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:14.840
The fact that he was apparently batching his
cameos naturally left the possibility that

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:18.400
he could be popping into films for an indefinite
amount of time.

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:23.560
Endgame delivered a shot of a younger-looking
hippie version of Lee driving by the 1970's

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:24.560
S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:26.580
headquarters shouting, "Make love, not war!"

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:30.960
As it turned out, that scene will be Lee's
last ever cameo, and it's a fitting farewell.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:35.600
The Russos confirmed after the fact that it's
the last Stan Lee cameo that made it to film.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.140
In a twist that few people saw coming in Endgame,
Natasha Romanoff ended up sacrificing herself

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:41.960
for the Soul Stone.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.289
It was a beautifully redemptive way to end
her own heroic story, which had been going

00:04:46.289 --> 00:04:49.020
on in the background for nearly the entire
MCU.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:53.240
But, of course, this is Marvel, and there's
always the question of whether they'll bring

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:53.740
her back.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:58.860
With Gamora's re-entrance into the future
from the past in Endgame, anything is possible.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:02.360
Except, not with Romanoff her story seems
closed for good this time.

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:07.020
Screenwriters Christopher Markus and Stephen
McFeely explained to The New York Times:

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:11.280
"Her journey, in our minds, had come to an
end if she could get the Avengers back.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.580
[...] She has a chance to get [them] back,
that's a thing she would trade for."

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:19.040
During her memorial scene on the dock, the
surviving heroes' conversation drove home

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:21.600
the point that the cost of the Soul Stone
is a permanent one.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:26.160
While there's no doubt that Black Widow will
be remembered by fans for years to come, she's

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.360
gone for good except, presumably, for her
upcoming movie.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.610
While Black Widow may have ultimately been
given a powerful closing to her own story,

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.040
Romanoff's death wasn't always a sure thing.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.820
After all, movies aren't completely finished
until the release date.

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:43.800
The shawarma end credit scene from Avengers
wasn't even added until the week that the

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:44.620
film was released.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:48.360
So it should come as no surprise that there
was a lot of shifting and changing as they

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.360
went along.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:50.360
One of those changes?

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:51.320
The events on Vormir.

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:54.840
Writers Markus and McFeely explained that
there was a version of the script that had

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:58.220
Clint Barton sacrificing himself for the stone
instead of Romanoff.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:02.520
But when they gave the scene to the visual
effects producer Jen Underdahl, she apparently

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.500
told them:

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:04.420
"Don't you take this away from her."

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:09.020
The response clearly impacted the writers,
who ultimately acknowledged that it had to

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:09.520
be Romanoff.

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:14.240
It turns out that Markus and McFeely have
had a working "master document" since Captain

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:17.410
America: Civil War, where they kept track
of different things they wanted to do with

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:18.300
their heroes' character arcs.

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:21.920
It turns out that Endgame was the movie where
they got to run wild.

00:06:22.729 --> 00:06:27.060
In particular, McFeely said that they wanted
to explore Marvel's tradition of "What If"

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.920
comics alternate universe presentations of
Marvel events playing out differently.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:35.200
The writers dove in and incorporated a bunch
of "what if" scenarios right into the Endgame

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:35.700
plot.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:37.340
The writers told The New York Times:

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:38.760
"Well, this is our What If?

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:40.440
If you lost, Thor becomes fat.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:41.900
Natasha becomes a shut-in.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:43.200
Steve becomes depressed.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:44.840
Tony gets on with his life.

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:46.480
Hulk is a superhero.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:48.500
[...] Clint becomes a murdering maniac."

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:53.240
While there were solid reasons for every character's
shift in tone, there's no doubt that the writers

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:56.520
were happy to push the heroes out of their
comfort zones for the entertainment of all.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:01.020
Infinity War and Endgame may be two distinctly
different movies, but the subject matter that

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:03.460
they handle is clearly interwoven.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:08.180
This left the writers and directors constantly
scrambling to figure out how to puzzle a bunch

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:12.490
of different pieces of storyline together
in a coherent way that left them with two

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:13.820
distinct yet connected movies.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:18.180
At one point, the plan was to have Hulk and
Banner resolve their differences in Infinity

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:18.680
War.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.760
However, it felt like too much of a positive
in the middle of a pretty sad ending, so the

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:24.560
filmmakers bumped it to Endgame.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:29.020
They also left out the actual transformation,
banking on Scott Lang in the diner scene to

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:31.320
provide the right amount of confusion to help
bridge the gap.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:36.280
In the same vein, Hawkeye's opening scene
in Endgame where he loses his family was nearly

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:39.620
tacked onto the end of Infinity War, but it
once again felt out of place.

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:42.540
The biggest decision of all was where to place
the Snap.

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:46.600
They debated putting it earlier in Infinity
War, but it would have left almost nothing

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:47.100
for the ending.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:51.420
On the other hand, if they pushed it to Endgame,
it would have left the first movie with no

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:52.160
ending at all.

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:55.660
Knowing when to reverse the Snap was nearly
as challenging.

00:07:55.669 --> 00:07:59.720
Thankfully, the creative minds behind the
scenes managed to eventually pull it all together

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:00.940
into two coherent stories.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:04.160
Scott Lang was right Endgame is a time heist
movie.

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:08.980
It wasn't the writers who came up with that
one, though, but Marvel boss Kevin Feige himself.

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:13.020
Feige's original suggestion was to use time
travel to resolve the Infinity War plot, with

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:15.040
the centerpiece being the Time Stone.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:19.460
It wasn't until the filmmakers began fleshing
things out that the Quantum Realm became the

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:22.080
key plot device to move the time travel element
forward.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:26.480
Once time travel was in the mix, there was
one obvious point that had to make it into

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:28.000
the film: the Battle of New York.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:33.280
But the writers initially felt it was too
"pander-y" to head to the original Avengers

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.620
film, so they tried a ton of alternatives
first.

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:40.390
At one point, they had Tony Stark on Asgard
in a stealth suit fighting Heimdall, and the

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:41.840
planet Morag was originally underwater.

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:46.700
At another point, the Avengers stopped by
Triskelion and even hopped in a car to visit

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:47.200
Dr. Strange.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:51.300
When the rubber hit the road, though, writers
and directors alike finally decided that heading

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:55.540
back to the Chitauri-infested streets of New
York was the best option of the bunch.

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:59.980
The sequence in Endgame when our heroes put
their heist plan to the test shows that time

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:04.020
travel within the MCU is distinctly different
than the way it's been represented in movies

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:07.200
like Time Cop, Hot Tub Time Machine, or Back
to the Future.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:07.900
"Doc.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:14.970
Are you telling me that you built a time machine
out of a DeLorean?"

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:18.760
It also establishes that Pym particles are
in extremely limited supply.

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:23.520
Tony Stark and Steve Rogers head back on a
potentially one-way trip to the '70s in the

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:27.770
hope of scrounging up more particles just
to keep everything from falling apart.

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:32.030
This left fans scratching their heads when
Nebula casually brought Thanos' entire planet-sized

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:34.020
ship and its massive army into the present.

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:38.320
Where on Earth did they get the impossibly
huge number of particles to pull off a stunt

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:38.820
like that?

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:43.480
Joe Russo cleared up the disconnect in an
interview, offering an elegantly simple solution.

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:49.200
When 2014 Nebula surrenders the Pym particles
to her father, they aren't for him to use

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:51.460
to time travel; they're for scientific research.

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:56.410
Thanos, a genius himself, with the help of
the powerful sorcerer Ebony Maw, studied the

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:59.860
Pym particles and then mass-produced them
in order to defeat the Avengers once and for

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:00.360
all.

